DSSI VMScluster Systems for DEC 7000 and 10000

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Mixed VAX and Alpha AXP DSSI VMScluster configurations now supported for
   DEC 7000 and 10000 systems

o  KFMSB adapter for DEC 7000 and 10000 systems is counterpart of KFMSA
   adapter for VAX 7000 and 10000 systems

o  KFMSB-UA upgrades KFMSA to KFMSB capabilities; used when upgrading VAX
   7000 or 10000 to DEC 7000 or 10000

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

A DSSI (Digital Storage Systems Interconnect) VMScluster configuration can
consist of VAX and Alpha AXP systems sharing RF disks across DSSI adapters,
using embedded XMI-to-DSSI adapters on each system. A backup and/or load
device (which can be shared by all systems), at least one Ethernet interface
on each system, and OpenVMS AXP with DECnet and VMScluster licenses on each
system are required.

Digital's VMScluster systems based on DSSI devices have a low entry cost but
provide the  same features as VMScluster systems based on CI devices,
including high availability, resource sharing, and cost effective system
management. (CI based VMScluster systems also offer enhanced scalability,
both in computing power and in storage connectivity.) The clusters have a
bandwidth of approximately 4.0 Mbytes per second.

The KFMSB adapter for disk and tape Integrated Storage Elements is an
intelligent, XMI based, dual-channel adapter between the XMI backplane bus
and two DSSI cable buses. It can support up to 14 RF72, RF73, RF74, RF35, or
RF36 disk storage elements or a combination of RF disk drives and TF8x/TF8xx
series magazine tape subsystems. The KFMSB supports up to four of the TF
Integrated Storage Elements (two per DSSI bus). It is available on Alpha AXP
based DEC 7000 and DEC 10000 systems and is the follow-on to its VAX
counterpart, the KFMSA-BA adapter.

Initially the KFMSB will offer the features shown in the left column of
Table 1. The next release of firmware, planned for late this year, will
provide the performance enhancements and other features listed in the right
column.

