Embedded HSD05 DSSI-to-SCSI Controller for VAX 4000

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Supports variety of Digital SCSI devices within VAX 4000-500A/600A/700A
   system enclosure and B400x expander for both pedestal and rackmount
   systems

o  Field upgrades for older models available; controller protects your
   investments in VAX 4000 systems and DSSI based OpenVMS clusters

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

The HSD05 is a DSSI-to-SCSI controller that mounts inside the VAX 4000
enclosure and lets you configure a variety of Digital's industry standard
SCSI disks, tapes, and CDROMs in VAX 4000 pedestal and rackmount systems.

It supports up to seven SCSI devices and can be installed with them at
initial order in VAX 4000 Model 500A, 600A, and 700A system enclosures and
the B400X expander. It can also be field installed in VAX 4000 Model 300 to
700 and 300A to 700A system enclosures, in the "big-box" VAX 4000 Model 200
(BA430) system enclosure, and in the B400X expander. Additional variants
have been created for rackmounted systems.

HSD05 features:

o  Direct connection to the DSSI bus.
o  Expands DSSI capacity: up to seven SCSI devices per DSSI node, and eight
   DSSI nodes per DSSI controller. When connected to the DSSI bus, the HSD05
   represents one node to the DSSI controller.
o  Supports host based Raid 0 (striping) and Raid 1 (unassisted host based
   volume shadowing).
o  Multihost operations: up to three hosts on a DSSI bus.
o  Up to 500 I/O requests per second and a transfer rate of 1.6 Mbytes per
   second.

For further information on the HSD05 controller, see the "StorageWorks HSD05
Array Controller" article in the March 14, 1994, issue of Digital's Customer
Update.

BENEFITS

The HSD05 brings the low cost of SCSI devices to the VAX 4000 family,
protecting your investments in DSSI OpenVMS clustering technology. You can
continue to enjoy the benefits of OpenVMS clustering -- such as a single
point of system management and shared storage across multiple CPUs -- while
gaining the flexibility of a wide variety of industry standard SCSI devices.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The HSD05 is orderable now for shipments beginning June 10, 1994 (the lead
time is 19 days).

Order No.	 Description

		 HSD05 Controller and SCSI Devices:
HSD05-JA/JF	 DSSI-to-SCSI controller embedded in pedestal enclosure for
                 BA440, BA430, or B400X; factory/field installed
HSD05-JG/JH	 DSSI-to-SCSI controller embedded in rackmount enclosure for
                 BA441 or B401X-A9; factory/field installed
RZ26L-AA/AF	 1.05 GBF 3.5" disk; factory/field installed
RZ262-AA/AF	 Two 1.05 GBF 3.5" disks; factory/field installed; use one
                 storage bay for 2.1 GB total
RZ28E-AA/AF	 2.1 GB 3.5" disk; factory/field installed
RZ282-AA/AF	 Two 2.1 GB 3.5" disks; factory/field installed; use one
                 storage bay for 4.2 GB total
TZ86-JA/JF	 6 GB 5.25" SCSI digital linear tape; factory/field
                 installed
TZ87-JA/JF	 20 GB 5.25" SCSI digital linear tape; factory/field
                 installed
RRD43-JA/JF	 600 MB 5.25" SCSI dual-speed CDROM; factory/field
                 installed

TECHNICAL DATA

Constraints

The SCSI bus created by the use of the embedded HSD05 is limited to the
storage bays internal to the BA440 or BA430 enclosure. Up to seven SCSI disk
drives are supported on the same SCSI bus from the HSD05, or one SCSI tape
drive and five SCSI disk drives.

Although the embedded HSD05 does not use the Q-bus, it does occupy one Q-bus
slot, reducing the Q-bus slots available by one.

If the KFDDB option is added, one of its connecting bulkheads can mount in
the same Q-bus slot as the one occupied by the HSD05. In effect, the one
Q-bus slot is occupied by the HSD05 and the additional DSSI connections
created by the use of the KFDDB option.

Please keep in mind the following configuration rules:

o  Tapes cannot be seen on the HSD05 when connected to the KFQSA controller.

o  The TZ86 drive cannot be used in TZ85 mode on writes from BOT (the TZ86
   will be able to read tapes written on a TZ85 or written in TZ85 mode by a
   TZ86).

o  For volume shadowing, all devices must be controlled by the HSD05 and the
   shadow sets must consist of the same SCSI device type; DSSI and SCSI
   drives can not be mixed, nor can RZ28 and RZ28B drives.

o  Mixing DSSI devices and SCSI devices inside the BA440 or BA430 enclosure
   is supported, except for volume shadowing.

Operating System Support

The OpenVMS VAX V5.5-2, V5.5-2H4, V6.0, and V6.1 releases are supported.
Limitations: The HSD05 DSSI-to-SCSI controller returns a limited set of
device type identification names to the host. All attached tapes are
identified as TU81 (MUAxxx). All attached disks are normally identified as
RF72 (DIAxxx).

DSSI Controllers Supported

o  EDA670, VAX 4000 internal DSSI.
o  D4000, DEC 4000 internal DSSI.
o  KFDDA, VAX 4000-100 DSSI option.
o  KFDDB, VAX 4000-5/6/700 DSSI option.
o  KFMSA, XMI to DSSI.
o  KFQSA, Q-bus to DSSI.



