 SPRINGFIELD PUBLIC SCHOOLS SELECTS DIGITAL TO NETWORK THEIR STUDENT
 LABS.....The Springfield Public Schools, located in Springfield,
 Massachusetts recently selected Digital to provide the network and
 integration services for systems purchased in support of their middle
 school science curriculum. The school system will have 46 science
 classrooms in all middle and K through 8 schools. Each classroom will
 be equipped with six student workstations in each lab. Eight teacher
 workstations, eight systems to be used as CDROM servers, and eight
 systems to be used as file servers were also purchased for each school.
 The systems will be used for math and science applications, for their
 standard CBI curriculum, and for full motion video and animation clips.
 The hardware solution will service approximately 6,000 students a year.

 * Contact - Robert Dionne, Science Department Director, Springfield
   Public Schools, (413) 787-7178

 CARDIFF UNIVERSITY USING ALPHA AXP'S TO ASSIST IN THEIR DRUG
 RESEARCH.... Working with computer programs running on an Alpha AXP
 computer, a team of researchers from Cardiff University's Department of
 Chemistry is now able to investigate how molecules react together. This
 will be deployed by the pharmaceutical industry to provide important
 insights into drug behavior. The team is using an Alpha AXP 300 Model
 400 workstation to process data and run programs for quantum chemistry
 and crystallographic calculations. Existing computer programs, and new
 ones developed by the team, process enormous amounts of data, and the
 Alpha AXP system has enabled the researchers to complete high-power
 computer work quickly in the laboratory. Previously, they had to queue
 for batch time on national supercomputer facilities. Professor Mike
 Hursthouse says that Alpha AXP gives his team near-supercomputer power
 in the laboratory for the first time, making it more feasible to plan
 and implement computationally-intensive programs which will contribute
 to the body of research work coming out of the department.

 * Contact: Professor Mike Hursthouse, Chemical Crystallographer,
   Cardiff University; hursthouse@cardiff.ac.uk

