 news0607.txt; Digital Internet Security Services Fact Sheet and Q&A

 DIGITAL EQUIPMENT CORPORATION- FACT SHEET

 PRODUCT/SERVICE NAME:

 Internet Security Services

 DESCRIPTION:

 Internet Security Services are customized consulting services to help
 clients ensure a high level of security between their private networks
 and the Internet global network and other public networks. The premier
 component of these services is SEAL (Screening External Access Link), a
 combination of expert security consulting and software capabilities to
 deliver a programmable "firewall" through a screened intelligent
 gateway to guard private networks while enabling users to have
 controlled links to Internet and other networks.  These services can
 also be used to protect sensitive areas of internal networks.

 FEATURES:

 SEAL (Screening External Access Link) - a combination of:

 *  Custom security consulting
 *  Installation and configuration of customized software
 *  Training in all facets of SEAL's operation
 *  Post-delivery telephone support

 Optional Components and Consulting:

 *  Internet security policy development and rules definitions
 *  Additional customized application gateways
 *  Configuration of public domain software
 *  Cryptographic and authentication capabilities
 *  Computer and network security consulting

 BENEFITS:

 *  A comprehensive security solution from a single source -- combining
    industry leading consulting, software, and hardware tailored to each
    customer's unique needs

 *  Fully "programmable" firewalls coupled with screened intelligent
    gateways, providing the highest possible levels of protection while
    allowing users to have controlled access to the Internet and other
    networks

 *  The assurance of unparalleled, hands-on experience in designing and
    building secure "firewalls" -- for Digital and customers. Digital's
    own firewall has secured the company's internal Easynet network for
    more than 5 years, managing an average of 1.7 million mail messages
    per month

 *  SEAL security solutions are tested and currently protecting numerous
    large and multinational customers

 PRICING:

 Internet Security Services are custom quoted.

 AVAILABILITY:

 Internet Security Services are available immediately in the United
 States, Canada, Latin America and Europe, and will be available in Asia
 in late 1994.

 Internet Security Services Announcement
 Questions and Answers

 Q.  How long does the telephone support last?

 A.  This support is provided for two weeks. Customers can renew, and
     pricing is quoted individually.

 Q.  Where in Digital do SEAL clients call for support?

 A.  They call Digital's Customer Support Centers.

 Q.  What is a "programmable" firewall and why is it of value?

 A.  A "programmable", or customized, firewall allows you to tailor the
     security solution to your individual organization's and users'
     needs; you decide who has access to what networks, both inbound and
     outbound.

 Q.  Is this the only "programmable" firewall in the industry?

 A.  No. However, Digital offers a turnkey solution and one of the most
     sophisticated and comprehensive security solutions.

 Q.  What is a "screened intelligent gateway" and what does it do?

 A.  This is a gateway between TCP/IP networks that uses various
     reference and security tables to allow or disallow access or
     connections through the gateway. The "intelligence" involves the
     use of prior knowledge or security clearance to determine
     accessibility.

 Q.  Who is your primary competition?

 A.  There are no major competitors, though several small firms offer
     security services, as do independent consultants with security
     expertise. However none offers the breadth of experience and such
     complete solutions, from a single source.

 Q.  Will Digital be licensed to sell Mosaic, and if so when?

 A.  We're currently talking with a number of organizations. Stay tuned.

 Q.  Does SEAL represent Digital's overall Internet strategy?

 A.  SEAL is one major component of serving the needs of companies
     wanting to connect to the Internet. Thanks to Digital's years of
     extensive experience testing and using the Internet, we have the
     expertise necessary to support this and other aspects of using the
     Internet.

     As demonstrated by the recent Commercenet announcement (see May
     DECNEWS article for details), Digital is committed to working with
     customers to determine the products and services needed to respond
     to the explosion of Internet popularity. Digital will continually
     explore and evolve our services and products related to the
     Internet as our customers' needs evolve, and will announce these as
     they become available.

 Q.  How many customer SEAL installations have you delivered?

 A.  More than two dozen large projects -- some international in scope
     -- with many more in the pipeline.

     | In Europe, Digital has completed SEAL installations in Sweden,
     | Germany, and the UK covering companies and organizations in a
     | variety of industries such as manufacturing, telecommunications,
     | aerospace, oil & gas, pharmaceuticals, and higher education.
     | There are many more opportunities throughout Europe currently in
     | the pipeline.

 Q.  How many consultants are available to deliver these consulting
     services?

 A.  We're currently ramping up the resources necessary to meet the
     anticipated business demands, and will draw from the Digital
     Consulting community worldwide. (NOTE: Additional resources are
     available in Europe from Digital's European Security Expertise
     Centre in the UK).

 Q.  How large is the market for these services?

 A.  Considering the Internet's phenomenal growth, this opens up an
     enormous, exploding opportunity. Everyone wanting connections to
     the Internet or needing a controlled internal subnet should be
     considering this type of security.

 Q.  What is the profile of your typical customers for these services?
     Are they more likely to be major companies, or any size business?

 A.  Any company or organization that wants to protect itself from
     Internet intruders. Customers in every industry, and of every size,
     are concerned about security today. The profile is as varied as the
     customers Digital supports.

 Q.  How will these services be marketed and sold by Digital?

 A.  Through direct sales, consultative selling and, eventually,
     appropriate channels such as Internet service providers.

 Q.  Do you have any customer references?

 A.  As you can well understand, the sensitivity of the solutions
     prevents our customers from serving as references. We have
     successfully implemented SEAL solutions in a variety of industries
     -- including government, financial institutions, large
     petrochemicals, health care and manufacturing -- and in a broad
     range of complex environments.

 Q.  How much revenue does Digital expect from Internet Security
     Services?

 A.  This is clearly a vibrant market with phenomenal opportunity, and
     we expect to be very successful.  However, Digital does not share
     its business plans.

 Q:  How long does it take to implement a SEAL solution?

 A:  Perhaps the better question is how long does it take to secure a
     networked environment. Digital works with its clients to deliver as
     comprehensive and customized a solution as necessary, to provide
     the highest level of security -- with the Internet and, if required
     and requested, throughout the customer's internal network.

     For typical SEAL solutions, provided the customer already has the
     necessary equipment, SEAL can be delivered in one or two weeks.

 Q.  Can SEAL run in any customer environment?

 A.  Yes. Regardless of the IP environment, we can put SEAL in front of
     it and secure it!

 Q:  What operating systems are required for the gateway?

 A:  SEAL solutions currently run on either ULTRIX 4.4 or OSF/1 2.0.

 Q:  Will you be going to other operating platforms, i.e., HP/UX, SunOS,
     etc.?

 A:  There are plans to expand to other popular operating platforms.
     However this will be driven by customer demand.

 Q:  What about a customer who has an OpenVMS environment?

 A:  The SEAL gateway will support an OpenVMS environment since the only
     machine that communicates with the Internet is the gateway.
     Therefore the customer platform is independent of the gateway.

 Q:  What is Digital's commitment to the continued engineering of SEAL
     and its futures?

 A:  Digital is committed to responding to our customers' needs -- and
     SEAL is a superb example of this. We will continue to evolve our
     products and services as the information highway expands and
     evolves.

 Q:  If I don't have an Internet connection will Digital get me one?

 A:  Yes. An authorized Internet connection is a prerequisite of SEAL,
     and we frequently help our customers get access to Internet.

 Q.  Does SEAL integrate with a customer's existing network?

 A.  Yes. SEAL can be used to protect any network. If the customer's
     existing network is IP based, then the integration is fairly
     simple. Integration with networks that don't speak the native
     Internet protocols can be accomplished through the use of relays
     that are a part of the two different network environments.

 Internet Security Services
 Supplementary Questions (Based on European Customer Input)

 Q.  Can the customer buy the SEAL software and install it?

 A.  NO, the SEAL solution is a combination of consulting expertise and
     software. The software is provided largely in source form and is
     not available separately.

 Q.  Much of the software is from the Public Domain, why can't customers
     build their own firewall?

 A.  Customers can build their own firewall based on Public Domain
     software, but some of the software used in the SEAL solution is
     proprietary to Digital and is not available externally, therefore,
     these components would be missing or other sources would need to be
     identified.

     If customers do decide to build their own firewalls, there are many
     issues to be addressed e.g. local expertise level, local support,
     compliance with your security requirements/policy, auditing,
     monitoring potential/actual attacks. Issues which can consume time
     and effort if the appropriate specialized kowledge is not readily
     available, in-house, to the customer.

 Q.  Internet Service Providers also offer a firewall via a commercial
     router option. How does the Digital offering differ?

 A.  Most commercial routers have limited configurability and little or
     no auditing/logging capability. Control is done by the firmware
     within the router - whether good or bad. If customers use a single
     router and this system is penetrated, the whole of the private
     network is at risk from attack! Users must consider the potential
     loss and the value of information to the business should such an
     attack succeed.

 Q.  Why does Digital propose 2 or 3 system firewall solutions when
     others suggest only single system solutions?

 A.  The Digital SEAL firewall solution can be run on 1, 2, 3 or more
     systems. Typically, we do not advise customers to use less than 2
     systems for security reasons - especially if application gateways
     are a requirement. In some cases these application gateways require
     users to login to the system.

     Permitting users to log in to the "single gateway" can be a
     significant risk - MOST successful penetrations of systems require
     a log in to that system.

     The multi-system approach means that the "gate" system can deny ALL
     logins EXCEPT specially nominated administration logins from known
     sources.

 Q.  Can users dial-in to a private network?

 A.  Yes, an optional service is available to provide dial-in capability
     to the external side of the gateway with secure authentication of
     user access via the usage of Hand-Held Authenticators (HHA) and a
     challenge-response system. This technology ensures that even if a
     username and password is "accidentally" discovered, a successful
     entry to the private network will be stopped since they cannot
     "trick" the HHA. Several types of HHA will be supported. Contact
     Digital for further information.

 Q.  Can external dial-up users run X-windows through the firewall?

 A.  Yes, an optional service is available to provide transparent
     X-windows transport through the firewall. This is configured
     typically in conjunction with the authenticated dial-in capability
     to maximize security.

 Q.  How does the user know if any of the components of the firewall
     configuration have been modified without their knowledge?

 A.  Digital provides a tool called the Polycenter Security Compliance
     Manager (available as a separate software option from Digital).
     This can be run on all systems, including those comprising the
     firewall. The software runs periodically and checks the
     configuration of the system against security requirements as
     defined in the operational Security Policy, Standard and Procedures
     documentation. Since the firewall contains elements of software and
     configuration details typically not present on all systems,
     customized tests can be produced to ensure compliance.

 Q.  How does the user know if the firewall is being attacked?

 A.  Digital provides a tool called the Polycenter Security Intrusion
     Detector (available as a separate software option from Digital).
     This tool runs continuously on the systems and alerts nominated
     individuals or groups of people if unexpected attempts to access a
     system is detected. Subsequently, if the attempts continue, the
     software can be configured to cause the system to take evasive
     action.

 Q.  How much additional systems management load will the SEAL firewall
     need?

 A.  Typically, once the firewall is installed, it takes less than 1 man
     day per week for management. If firewall reconfiguration is
     necessary at a later date due to changes in requirements, this will
     take additional effort.

 Q.  How much disk space is necessary for logging information from the
     firewall?

 A.  This is an open question, but for a period of 3 months the disk
     space requirement is often under 10MB. It depends on how much
     logging is required, how long the information needs to be retained,
     and how much other processing data must be kept.



