 news0608.txt; - Digital StorageWorks HSZ40 Array Controller Fact Sheet

 HIGHLIGHTS

 o  The HSZ40 Array Controller increases locally attached SCSI disk
    connectivity and extends I/O performance with RAID 0 (Striping)
    standard and optional Read Cache in 16 or 32 MB capacities

 o  A maximum of 36 to 42 industry standard SCSI-2 disk devices may be
    added via the HSZ40 to a single Fast-Wide Differential SCSI
    (FWD-SCSI) port with up to 16 Logical Units addressed by the
    controller -- as with the HSJ40, device attachment is supported by
    six independent SCSI-2 buses

 o  Supported on DEC OSF/1 for AXP V2.0, with future OpenVMS AXP planned

 o  Available now

 PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

 With 36 to 42 industry standard SCSI-2 disk devices maximum, the HSZ40
 is supported in the standard BA350-MA/MB shelf for SW500 and SW800
 series enclosures. Connection to device shelves is accomplished with
 six SCSI cables. The HSZ40 optionally permits configuration in a Dual
 Target mode on the SCSI bus. Appearing as two SCSI targets, the number
 of Logical Units supported by the HSZ40 is doubled from eight to
 sixteen. Certain configuration restrictions may apply however.

 Initially, the following devices are supported on the HSZ40:

 RZ26        RZ28        EZ51R
 RZ26L       RZ28B       EZ54R
                         EZ58R

 The tall SW800 data center cabinet supports up to eighteen building
 block shelves and four controller shelves. The SW500 cabinet can hold a
 total of 10 shelves, including one or two controller shelves.

 The HSZ40 controller enables significant disk connectivity expansions
 through multiple host adapters. It uses the same industry-standard
 StorageWorks devices and packaging that are supported across a growing
 number of Digital and non Digital platforms. It meets the needs of
 customers who have asked for scalable storage solutions that are
 supported across multiple platforms using industry-standard devices and
 common interconnects.  It also meets the needs of those customers who
 asked for continued access to once-proprietary features, improved
 levels of storage availability and better price/performance in an open
 environment.

 To provide balanced I/O performance, HSZ40-based RAID 0 and Read Cache
 can also be deployed.

 BENEFITS

 o  High Capacity -- Scalable solutions provide up to 88 GB of disk
    storage on a single HSZ40.

 o  High Performance -- HSZ40-based RAID 0 is standard for increasing
    I/O performance in both throughput- and bandwidth-intensive
    environments. Optional Read Cache in 16 or 32 MB increments is
    supported, providing significant improvements in subsystem latency
    for many applications. And the HSZ40 has the internal performance to
    support high I/O activity, with up to 1400 I/O/s request rates and
    8-10 MB/s bandwidth.

 o  High Availability -- For high availability systems, redundant power
    options are available for device and controller shelves and the
    various StorageWorks enclosures.

 o  A wide range of supported devices enables users to tailor
    performance and capacity using the drives and media best suited to
    their applications.

 MIGRATION STRATEGIES

 Customers who own supported processor platforms can add HSZ40
 capability via the KZMSA (XMI) or KZTSA (TURBOchannel) adapters. The
 KZMSA attachment requires the DWZZA SCSI adapter.

 Disk devices may also be migrated from HSZ10, HSC/K.SCSI and the HSJ
 and HSD-Series subsystems.

 SYSTEM SUPPORT

 The HSZ40 Array Controller is supported by DEC OSF/1 V2.0, with future
 OpenVMS AXP support planned. In configuring the HSZ40 Array Controller,
 all devices on a SCSI bus occupied by the HSZ40 must be attached via
 the controller, with boot devices attached to other buses. Processor
 platform and adapter support is shown below, along with platform-
 specific configuration guidelines.

 DEC OSF/1 V2.0   Configuration Guidelines

 DEC 3000         KZTSA        	 Up to 3 HSZ40 Array Controllers
 		  		 supported per KZTSA; Dual Target
 		  		 feature supported.

 DEC 7000/10000   KZMSA/DWZZA  	 A maximum of 1 HSZ40 per KZMSA bus (2
 		  		 total per KZMSA); Dual Target feature
 		  		 not supported.

 To support over eight physical devices (or sixteen devices with the
 Dual Target feature deployed) on an HSZ40 will require logical unit
 configurations using the RAID 0 (striping) capability of the
