DCE FOR OpenVMS V1.2

The DCE for OpenVMS product family provides the OpenVMS implementation of
the distributed computing functionality specified by the Open Software
Foundation (OSF). The DCE is a comprehensive, integrated set of services and
tools that support the creation, use, and maintenance of client/server
applications in heterogeneous, multivendor environments.

Using the DCE, application programmers can design and build client/server
applications that run over a variety of operating systems and networks. They
simply code to the DCE Remote Procedure Call, using a set of standardized
APIs to reap the benefits of all the underlying DCE components. The DCE also
masks from the application programmer the complexities and differences
between the underlying network, operating systems, and hardware platforms.
The programmers no longer need to worry about network protocols or data
representation while passing data among heterogeneous computing systems. The
DCE makes network elements transparent to the programmer and helps makes
multivendor interoperability and application portability a reality.

As the DCE is implemented on virtually every major industry platform,
programmers can design and build DCE applications to run in almost any
multivendor environment. Digital provides support for the DCE on its
OpenVMS, DEC OSF/1, and (later this year) Windows and Windows NT platforms.
For further information please see the "Distributed Computing Environment
for OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1" article in the February 14, 1994, issue of
Digital's Customer Update and the "Distributed Computing Environment (DCE)
for Windows NT" article in the April 12, 1994, issue.

The new features in DCE for OpenVMS V1.2 (SPD/SSA 43.05.02) are as follows:

o  DCE Security Server -- Provides secure communications and access via
   authorization and authentication services.

o  DCE Cell Directory Server (CDS) -- Provides location independent naming
   for resources.

o  Global Directory Agent (GDA) -- The Global Directory Agent provides a
   means of linking multiple CDS namespaces via the Internet Domain Name
   Server (BIND).

o  Code upgrades -- All DCE for OpenVMS products have been upgraded to the
   OSF DCE 1.0.3 code base.

o  NSedit -- This Motif based graphical tool enables users to navigate,
   manipulate, and peruse the CDS name space.

Key benefits:

o  Digital has the most comprehensive DCE offering in the industry: it
   supports DCE on OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 systems today and will support DCE
   on PCs in the future. Note that the OpenVMS operating system, available
   on both the VAX and Alpha AXP platforms, is one of the first non-UNIX
   operating systems to provide full DCE core services.

o  The OpenVMS operating system is currently the only one that licenses the
   DCE Runtime Services layered product with the operating system at no
   additional cost. Retroactive to OpenVMS VAX V5.5-2 and OpenVMS AXP V1.5,
   this is the only operating system that comes DCE-ready, combining open,
   standards based distributed computing technologies with the high
   integrity, feature rich OpenVMS computing environment.

o  The combination of DCE and OpenVMS strengths makes this operating system
   a highly reliable, available, and scalable system, ideal for
   client/server computing.

o  Digital's DCE products are OSF certified and interoperate with other
   vendors' OSF certified DCE implementations

The DCE for OpenVMS product family for consists of several layered products,
each licensed separately to provide users with maximum flexibility for
configuring a DCE environment, known as a DCE "cell." The products are as
follows:

o  DCE Runtime Services, which is required for all systems participating in
   the DCE cell -- The DCE Runtime Services kit includes DCE client
   functions as well as DCE administration tools. The right-to-use DCE
   Runtime Services is included with the OpenVMS operating system. Please
   note that media and documentation for this product are ordered and
   shipped separately from the OpenVMS operating system.

o  DCE Application Development kit, which is required for developers of
   distributed applications -- The kit provides programmers with an
   Interface Definition Language (IDL), an easy-to-use, ANSI C based
   language for writing remote procedure calls.

o  DCE Cell Directory Server, at least one of which is required for each DCE
   cell -- The DCE CDS is a central repository containing information about
   the location of resources in the DCE cell. It allows access to resources
   by a single name, regardless of physical location.

o  DCE Security Server, at least one of which is required for each DCE cell
   -- This server protects resources from illegal access and provides secure
   communications within and between DCE cells.

DCE for OpenVMS V1.2 is available now. The documentation kits listed below
are the same for both the VAX and Alpha AXP platforms. Both media kits
